# Module name e.g. myscript.sol
# would become myscript module name, can be included with myscript = use "myscript". if its in the current dir or package
# otherwise specify use "myscript#hash" to get the "non local namespace" which has a certain resolution order: cache -> registry
# searches cache first, typically ~/.sol/cache, if not there, go to ~/.sol/registry.json and go to the registries specified and look through them
# If there is a local sol.json in the current dir/package it would actually look at that first

# in the repl it should look like with bindings
# So it does > which means x = with (...)
# so when you do x = ...
# that means x = with ...

# big deals are the Map with the string traversals and Results everywhere
# Files are all linear so you cant "forget" to use them
# dont have to use with if you just start off with a single linear producer
# if you have multiple then you kind of have to

# read is not expected to fail, just either block due to lock or etc.

HOME = std.home_dir (). # call the function, might change so needs an actual function

# f1 : (File 1, Result a)
f1 = read' "{HOME}/a.json".
(f2, Ok b) = read' "{HOME}/b.json". # b now read, if PM fail then fail

default_file_error = error "Could not read file".

# manually extract
(f1, b) = case f1 of
  (File 1 f1, Ok a) -> (f1, a)
| _ -> default_file_error.

# shadow previous a
a = JSON.parse a?. # ? just means unwrap
b = JSON.parse b?.

# Lens -> setter for Map. Note set actually returns Result for Map. unwrap fails like the typical error "..."
merge a b = set a "authors" b.authors?.

c = merge a b.

# mostly care about smart pipeline |> here and linear types and STM on per file /home/stuff, which need to be tracked
# across the entire script
# because read and write both modify the read and write sets of the stuff of the Path that the File handle points to
# rather than the actual path

write' "{HOME}/x.json". # write' = write + close, so no need to manually close

close f1.
close f2. # if we dont close these handles somewhere, it should error at compile time

# Note, typical things are basically just Maps, Strings, Records
# custom types can be done but typically not recommended over builtin stuff and Result or Maybe types

# No if expressions, always use pattern matching.

# Using smart pipeline on the most common forms in statement context
# Note, uses the same read set from a.json above
read' "{HOME}/a.json" |> JSON.parse |> MyRecord.from |> set .author "Jack" |> write' "{HOME}/a.json".
# so using |> threads through File 1 and Result and just `unwraps` them if the next function expects a instead of Result
# If a thing returns File 1, it would also thread that through like normal, like a typical a type
